{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_1_rate_again": 2, "puzzle_1_guesses": "never", "puzzle_2_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_2_guesses": "never", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_3_guesses": "never", "puzzle_1_rating_reason": "If I remember correctly, there were many \"full\" columns near the sides that allowed me to build off of the first/last rows (or rows/columns switched). There were mostly just big chunks, too (not sparse) so it was fairly straightforward. I didn't give it a 1 because it wasn't the most straightforward that I have seen.", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "This puzzle was slightly harder than the first one because it had a few non-obvious \"full\" columns/rows due to more \"sparser\" groupings (i.e. 1 4 2 is not obviously a full column but it is). The rest of the puzzle was straightforward to work through building off of these.", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "This puzzle seemed easy at first with the two full 8 1 columns and rows, but the sparse and small chunks overall made it harder to keep track of where to look/check next. Many 1s and 2s kept a lot of uncertainty that couldn't be eliminated quickly, so I had to work through the puzzle with a lot of \"tolerance\" in mind. It was mostly an exercise of finding where to look next and keeping track of how one piece of information updates the other columns and rows, which is hard.", "strategy": "- Looking for full rows and columns first and marking all boxes I am sure are white\n- When filling end or near-end boxes, checking the corresponding row/column to fill their end boxes\n- For near-full rows, filling in the guaranteed boxes (e.g. for 4 4, the guaranteed boxes would be 2-4, 7-9)\n- For sparse columns, using X boxes to \"try\" fitting groups and seeing if I can eliminate other boxes from there (e.g. for something like 1 2 1 if a 2 can only fit the second half of the board, then that immediately tells me that the 1 after it must be int he second half too, usually can deduce more Xs from this)\n- When filling a row, immediately checking if that adds information to deduce for their columns (and vice versa).\n- Checking different rows and columns rapidly rather than fixating and trying to immediately solve a section or such", "comments": "These puzzles didn't have clear symmetry, which I suppose made them \"not easy\""}, "t": "2026-06-03T16:39:52.899462+00:00"}
